Third-party components in or used by HakiOS

In the repository:

- XNU (Apple): the kernel sources are fetched by xnu/apply.sh from github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu;
  our patch and files are a derivative work under the Apple Public Source License 2.0 (LICENSE.APSL).
- libdeflate (Eric Biggers), xnu/files/bsd/miscfs/apfs/libdeflate/: MIT, see its COPYING.
- mkbootimg.py (Android Open Source Project), devices/redwood/prebuilt/mkbootimg.py: Apache License 2.0.
- Mu-Silicium / Project Mu (Project Silicium, Microsoft and contributors): devices/redwood/uefi/ holds a
  patch and files against it; their license applies to the derived parts (BSD-2-Clause-Patent).
- UEFI driver binaries of the phone's own firmware, devices/redwood/uefi/files/Binaries/redwood/: the
  vendor's (Qualcomm/Xiaomi), extracted from the phone's xbl.img the way Project Silicium redistributes
  such drivers for other phones. They are needed only to rebuild the UEFI image; they can be removed and
  extracted by each user from their own phone's firmware with UEFIReader.

Fetched at build time, never committed:

- the iOS 16.5 IPSW (Apple): the user space of the system image; sysimage/fetch.sh downloads it from
  Apple's public servers; its license is Apple's.
- the iPhoneOS 16.5 SDK from github.com/theos/sdks, and theos's cctools (Apple's open-source cctools): the
  kernel's and user space's build tools.
- the phone's firmware (GPU, Wi-Fi, audio DSP, modem, haptics, cameras): the vendor's, pulled from the
  user's own phone (devices/redwood/pull-firmware.sh).
- lzfse (Apple, BSD-3-Clause), built by xnuloader/mkidentity.sh when no LZFSE decoder is installed.
- Mesa (for GL=1 images), Procursus and Sileo packages (tools/mk-jb.sh): their own licenses.
